00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:23.957
which you can apply for your
own organizations immediately.

00:03:23.957 --> 00:03:25.790
So let's talk about
some of those high level

00:03:25.790 --> 00:03:29.930
approaches first before we get
into a little bit more details.

00:03:29.930 --> 00:03:31.820
We talked about
those four stages

00:03:31.820 --> 00:03:35.100
of the customer migration
journey in the previous slide.

00:03:35.100 --> 00:03:38.780
These stages get repeated in
each phase of our migration

00:03:38.780 --> 00:03:40.030
approach.

00:03:40.030 --> 00:03:42.500
For the migration approach,
this is an example

00:03:42.500 --> 00:03:46.400
of how we view the world of
complete cloud migration,

00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:50.700
beginning from building the
foundational layer to migrating

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:54.680
the key workload to the cloud
as a hybrid architecture,

00:03:54.680 --> 00:03:58.010
and building the full data
center capability in GCP,

00:03:58.010 --> 00:04:00.710
and finally moving
data and retiring some

00:04:00.710 --> 00:04:03.020
of those on-prem data centers.

00:04:03.020 --> 00:04:06.490
Let's double lick
each one of those.

00:04:06.490 --> 00:04:11.030
Phase zero-- this is where we
build our core GCP foundational

00:04:11.030 --> 00:04:14.360
components and validate
key assumptions.

00:04:14.360 --> 00:04:16.910
The goal of this phase
is to build up components

00:04:16.910 --> 00:04:20.550
so that you can be ready
for future migration.

00:04:20.550 --> 00:04:23.360
Phase one is where we
migrate a selected workload

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:26.870
and build automations
around GCP deployment.

00:04:26.870 --> 00:04:29.720
This can be considered
as a hybrid architecture.

00:04:29.720 --> 00:04:33.080
The goal of this phase to ensure
that on-prem architecture works

00:04:33.080 --> 00:04:35.340
with the cloud and
begin to leverage

00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:38.560
clouds for the
selected workload.

00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:41.350
And phase two-- this is where
we build full data center

00:04:41.350 --> 00:04:45.010
capabilities in GCP by
replicating existing

00:04:45.010 --> 00:04:47.110
infrastructure architecture.

00:04:47.110 --> 00:04:49.330
The goal of this phase is
to build a complete set

00:04:49.330 --> 00:04:52.180
of the data center
capability in cloud

00:04:52.180 --> 00:04:56.140
so any backend support for
on-prem is no longer needed.

00:04:56.140 --> 00:04:58.020
And then obviously,
lastly, phase three

00:04:58.020 --> 00:05:02.610
is where we migrate on-prem
customers and data to GCP

00:05:02.610 --> 00:05:04.680
and retire the data center.

00:05:04.680 --> 00:05:07.890
One thing that we noticed
is not all customers

00:05:07.890 --> 00:05:10.680
will go through the complete
cloud migration journey.

00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:14.370
Rather, we commonly adopt
the hybrid architecture.

00:05:14.370 --> 00:05:16.440
Due to this reason, we
selected our lessons

00:05:16.440 --> 00:05:18.780
learned from our customers
who are currently

00:05:18.780 --> 00:05:21.060
in phase one and two for today.

00:05:25.120 --> 00:05:27.520
While we were executing
our migration approach,

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:31.290
we identified and selected
five common topics

00:05:31.290 --> 00:05:36.600
that we learned from our
customers for today's session.

00:05:36.600 --> 00:05:39.260
First, lift and shift
or lift and what?

00:05:39.260 --> 00:05:41.930
We'll discuss around the
beauty of the lift and shift

00:05:41.930 --> 00:05:44.510
and then the thrill
of transform.

00:05:44.510 --> 00:05:47.570
There are many shiny objects
and topics in the cloud.

00:05:47.570 --> 00:05:49.340
And we wanted to
share a few examples

00:05:49.340 --> 00:05:51.730
around how we address them.

00:05:51.730 --> 00:05:55.630
Second, data center
meets the cloud--

00:05:55.630 --> 00:05:57.940
one of the key benefits
of adopting cloud

00:05:57.940 --> 00:06:00.610
is managed services to
reduce the operation

00:06:00.610 --> 00:06:02.050
of infrastructure.

00:06:02.050 --> 00:06:05.260
For example, all the specific
versions of the open source

00:06:05.260 --> 00:06:07.570
may get supported as part
of the managed services.

00:06:07.570 --> 00:06:09.700
And we'll discuss that.

00:06:09.700 --> 00:06:12.160
Discoveries and
dependency-- our lessons

00:06:12.160 --> 00:06:14.740
learned from our
discovery process around,

00:06:14.740 --> 00:06:16.450
what are the downstream
dependencies?

00:06:16.450 --> 00:06:18.430
How are we going
to approach that?

00:06:18.430 --> 00:06:21.160
Deployment strategy--
we'll discuss around

00:06:21.160 --> 00:06:24.880
the short-term and long-term
strategy around automation

00:06:24.880 --> 00:06:28.060
and how we are deploying
infrastructure.

00:06:28.060 --> 00:06:31.030
To automate or not to
automate is the key question

00:06:31.030 --> 00:06:32.350
around that.

00:06:32.350 --> 00:06:35.590
Finally, the testing
approach, testing approach

00:06:35.590 --> 00:06:38.390
between hybrid and full stack--

00:06:38.390 --> 00:06:40.475
the decision on testing
approach due to service

00:06:40.475 --> 00:06:43.870
interdependency--
test in hybrid mode

00:06:43.870 --> 00:06:46.930
or doing individual unit
testing and putting it together

00:06:46.930 --> 00:06:49.420
for full integration testing?

00:06:49.420 --> 00:06:52.420
That being said,
I'll pass to Swati

00:06:52.420 --> 00:06:54.820
for additional details for
each topic for our lessons.

00:06:58.395 --> 00:06:59.770
SWATI LYENGAR:
Thank you, Dillon.

00:07:04.850 --> 00:07:06.110
All right.

00:07:06.110 --> 00:07:07.890
Hi everyone, nice to meet you.

00:07:07.890 --> 00:07:08.970
My name is Swati Lyengar.

00:07:08.970 --> 00:07:11.795
I'm a strategic cloud
engineer at Google.

00:07:11.795 --> 00:07:15.410
So I'm going to talk about
aspiration versus reality.

00:07:15.410 --> 00:07:18.290
So to start out
with, lift and shift

00:07:18.290 --> 00:07:21.410
can mean many different things
to many different people

00:07:21.410 --> 00:07:23.210
and organizations.

00:07:23.210 --> 00:07:27.020
For example, lift and shift
can be taking everything

00:07:27.020 --> 00:07:29.840
that you have in your
on-premise and plopping it

00:07:29.840 --> 00:07:32.510
right into the cloud.

00:07:32.510 --> 00:07:35.900
Lift and shift can mean taking
what you have in on-premises

00:07:35.900 --> 00:07:39.980
and taking advantage of
some services in the cloud,

00:07:39.980 --> 00:07:43.520
such as the managed services
that Dillon had mentioned,

00:07:43.520 --> 00:07:46.190
or some of our big
data solutions.

00:07:46.190 --> 00:07:49.040
Or lift and shift
can mean taking

00:07:49.040 --> 00:07:52.170
what you have in on-premises,
completely changing it,

00:07:52.170 --> 00:07:55.400
and replatforming it to take
advantage of a completely

00:07:55.400 --> 00:07:57.200
new technology stack.

00:07:57.200 --> 00:07:59.450
That being said, it could
be Kubernetes Engine,

00:07:59.450 --> 00:08:02.360
it could be App Engine, it could
be Managed Instance Groups.

00:08:02.360 --> 00:08:06.590
It just depends on how you want
to move your technology stack.

00:08:06.590 --> 00:08:10.260
So there's a lot of
different options here.

00:08:10.260 --> 00:08:13.190
It can have many
different meanings.

00:08:13.190 --> 00:08:16.670
That being said,
how do you decide

00:08:16.670 --> 00:08:18.170
which approach to
take when you're

00:08:18.170 --> 00:08:20.750
going through a lift and shift?

00:08:20.750 --> 00:08:24.260
You may look at your
existing on-premise stack

00:08:24.260 --> 00:08:27.260
and see how many different
moving parts there are.

00:08:27.260 --> 00:08:30.782
Maybe you have 80 or
hundreds of microservices.

00:08:30.782 --> 00:08:32.990
And so there's a lot of
different moving parts in how

00:08:32.990 --> 00:08:35.240
you're implemented today.

00:08:35.240 --> 00:08:38.929
And then you take a look
at your application stack.

00:08:38.929 --> 00:08:40.159
How complex is it?

00:08:40.159 --> 00:08:42.830
How many different
technologies is it using today

00:08:42.830 --> 00:08:44.090
in on-premises?

00:08:44.090 --> 00:08:47.150
And how will that
translate to the cloud?

00:08:47.150 --> 00:08:49.310
Are your applications ready?

00:08:49.310 --> 00:08:53.630
And then you gauge how
much uncertainty there is.

00:08:53.630 --> 00:08:56.060
Are you moving one
to two applications

00:08:56.060 --> 00:08:58.280
where you have a really
good understanding of what

00:08:58.280 --> 00:09:01.190
the entire stack
looks like and how

00:09:01.190 --> 00:09:03.260
that will look in the cloud?

00:09:03.260 --> 00:09:07.310
Or are you dealing with
hundreds of services,

00:09:07.310 --> 00:09:10.110
you may have some legacy stuff
sitting in the application

00:09:10.110 --> 00:09:12.080
stack, you're not
quite sure what

00:09:12.080 --> 00:09:14.840
that's going to look like
at the end of the day?

00:09:14.840 --> 00:09:18.155
The other input to
consider is timeline.

00:09:18.155 --> 00:09:21.650
Do you have a few months
to do this lift and shift?

00:09:21.650 --> 00:09:23.640
Or do you have a few years?

00:09:23.640 --> 00:09:25.590
And that can mean
many different things.

00:09:25.590 --> 00:09:28.580
And lastly, what's
your risk appetite?

00:09:28.580 --> 00:09:30.450
When you're making
this lift and shift,

00:09:30.450 --> 00:09:33.530
how much risk do you want to
take on initially considering

00:09:33.530 --> 00:09:36.920
all these different
factors and taking

00:09:36.920 --> 00:09:39.170
a look at your
technology aspirations

00:09:39.170 --> 00:09:41.720
and where you want to take
your technology stack?

00:09:41.720 --> 00:09:44.300
But then also what are
the business use cases?

00:09:44.300 --> 00:09:47.480
And how does that map to the
approach that you want to take?

00:09:47.480 --> 00:09:50.390
It could be a pure lift
issue to begin with, and then

00:09:50.390 --> 00:09:52.420
a transformation later on.

00:09:52.420 --> 00:09:57.910
Or it could be taking on the
whole transition all at once.

00:09:57.910 --> 00:10:01.360
All right, so let's take a look
at this example application

00:10:01.360 --> 00:10:04.090
stack that are customer had.

00:10:04.090 --> 00:10:06.740
And this is just
part of that stack.

00:10:06.740 --> 00:10:10.390
So they had 60 to
80 microservices

00:10:10.390 --> 00:10:15.550
in a global mesh,
60 to 80 per region.

00:10:15.550 --> 00:10:17.530
You're looking at a
full stack, including

00:10:17.530 --> 00:10:21.370
Apache Tomcat for the
frontend, Redis for caching,

00:10:21.370 --> 00:10:26.320
DataFlow, ZooKeeper in
MySQL for data storage,

00:10:26.320 --> 00:10:28.300
Hadoop, and much, much more.

00:10:28.300 --> 00:10:30.970
It was the entire kitchen sink.

00:10:30.970 --> 00:10:33.970
There was a lot in there.

00:10:33.970 --> 00:10:40.120
And given that there were
several microservices

00:10:40.120 --> 00:10:46.900
and a very complex
application stack,

00:10:46.900 --> 00:10:49.180
and a lot of different
moving pieces,

00:10:49.180 --> 00:10:52.010
they decided on the
approach to take a pure lift

00:10:52.010 --> 00:10:54.706
and shift as much as possible.

00:10:54.706 --> 00:10:56.580
Of course, there's always
differences, right?

00:10:56.580 --> 00:10:59.370
Because operating in
your on-premise data

00:10:59.370 --> 00:11:03.090
center is not exactly the same
as operating in the cloud.

00:11:03.090 --> 00:11:04.810
You're moving to
software defined

00:11:04.810 --> 00:11:09.100
networking versus having
hardware networking.

00:11:09.100 --> 00:11:10.700
You're looking at
other differences,

00:11:10.700 --> 00:11:13.360
such as the way your
access controls work

00:11:13.360 --> 00:11:15.730
and how the managed
services work.

00:11:15.730 --> 00:11:19.060
So there will be some
differences still.

00:11:19.060 --> 00:11:23.170
The pure lift and shift was
what this customer journey

00:11:23.170 --> 00:11:23.740
started with.

00:11:26.700 --> 00:11:28.860
So the data center
meeting the cloud--

00:11:28.860 --> 00:11:30.370
what does this mean?

00:11:30.370 --> 00:11:32.870
What does this look like when
you start this transformation,

00:11:32.870 --> 00:11:34.400
this journey?

00:11:34.400 --> 00:11:37.640
So to start with, there
is a few different things

00:11:37.640 --> 00:11:39.050
to think about.

00:11:39.050 --> 00:11:43.340
For example, when you're
moving to a managed service,

00:11:43.340 --> 00:11:44.810
it's beautiful, right?

00:11:44.810 --> 00:11:47.450
With the click of a
button, you get backups.

00:11:47.450 --> 00:11:49.880
You can, in the
case of Cloud SQL,

00:11:49.880 --> 00:11:52.700
easily configure read replicas.

00:11:52.700 --> 00:11:59.150
You can go ahead and
operate in multiple zones,

00:11:59.150 --> 00:12:05.000
have your data be held in
multiple zones for higher

00:12:05.000 --> 00:12:06.170
availability.

00:12:06.170 --> 00:12:07.820
And you have a SLAs.

00:12:07.820 --> 00:12:10.310
And someone's managing
the service for you.

00:12:10.310 --> 00:12:11.180
It's beautiful.

00:12:11.180 --> 00:12:13.340
It comes with all the
bells and whistles.

00:12:13.340 --> 00:12:16.280
But at the same time,
you need to evaluate

00:12:16.280 --> 00:12:20.300
how you're using that software
in your on-premises today

00:12:20.300 --> 00:12:23.300
and how is that going to fit
into this manage service.

00:12:23.300 --> 00:12:25.490
There may be a few differences.

00:12:25.490 --> 00:12:29.120
For one, there could be
differences in terms of maybe

00:12:29.120 --> 00:12:32.840
how much data you're holding
in one instance in on-premises

00:12:32.840 --> 00:12:38.150
and differences in the
maximum amount of data

00:12:38.150 --> 00:12:42.230
that can be held in an instance
in the managed service.

00:12:42.230 --> 00:12:43.880
There could be
differences in terms

00:12:43.880 --> 00:12:46.640
of how you're accessing
the service, in terms

00:12:46.640 --> 00:12:50.970
of how you can access
it as a managed service.

00:12:50.970 --> 00:12:53.840
So a lot of things
to think about when

00:12:53.840 --> 00:12:56.510
deciding your approach.

00:12:56.510 --> 00:12:58.160
What else does it mean?

00:12:58.160 --> 00:13:01.180
So in your on-premise
environment,

00:13:01.180 --> 00:13:03.740
it may have evolved
over time, where

00:13:03.740 --> 00:13:05.420
you had a number of things.

00:13:05.420 --> 00:13:07.550
Maybe for logging
and monitoring,

00:13:07.550 --> 00:13:09.620
you have your own
in-house solutions.

00:13:09.620 --> 00:13:13.040
You may be running
various other solutions

00:13:13.040 --> 00:13:18.800
like Check_MK, Nagios,
Splunk, Datadog, you name it.

00:13:18.800 --> 00:13:20.300
And then when you
move to the cloud,

00:13:20.300 --> 00:13:23.150
you may want to look at
everything that you're running

00:13:23.150 --> 00:13:26.810
and how you're configured to
troubleshoot in production

00:13:26.810 --> 00:13:28.750
today in on-premise
and think, "OK,

00:13:28.750 --> 00:13:31.910
if I'm going to centralize
my logging and my monitoring

00:13:31.910 --> 00:13:33.980
for ease of
troubleshooting, what

00:13:33.980 --> 00:13:35.810
is that going to look like?

00:13:35.810 --> 00:13:38.930
How am I going to make sure
that the format is the same

00:13:38.930 --> 00:13:42.500
and that all the logs that
were available in my on-premise

00:13:42.500 --> 00:13:45.900
are going to be there in the
centralized logging solution?"

00:13:45.900 --> 00:13:47.480
So there is a large
level effort that

00:13:47.480 --> 00:13:50.120
goes into that to
make sure that, aside

00:13:50.120 --> 00:13:55.130
from moving your services over,
you're operationally ready.

00:13:55.130 --> 00:13:57.560
The other piece
of it is, to reap

00:13:57.560 --> 00:14:01.890
the benefits of some of the
key features in the cloud--

00:14:01.890 --> 00:14:07.760
for example, auto scaling
of your resources and auto

00:14:07.760 --> 00:14:09.200
healing--

00:14:09.200 --> 00:14:12.830
you may be moving
to a platform that

00:14:12.830 --> 00:14:18.920
has dynamic IP addresses,
where, if an instance becomes

00:14:18.920 --> 00:14:21.980
unhealthy, for example, in
a managed instance group,

00:14:21.980 --> 00:14:24.650
let's say, the
managed instance group

00:14:24.650 --> 00:14:29.880
will go ahead and delete the
instance, and then recreate it.

00:14:29.880 --> 00:14:33.050
But it's going to come back
as a new virtual machine

00:14:33.050 --> 00:14:36.560
with a different IP address
out of a specified subnet,

00:14:36.560 --> 00:14:38.170
of course.

00:14:38.170 --> 00:14:42.020
And the question is, from
an operations perspective,

00:14:42.020 --> 00:14:43.440
are you are you ready for that?

00:14:43.440 --> 00:14:45.620
Are your troubleshooting
playbooks

00:14:45.620 --> 00:14:49.940
updated to be
comfortable with that?

00:14:49.940 --> 00:14:55.540
Or do you still have the need
to SSH into a virtual machine

00:14:55.540 --> 00:14:59.540
to do your troubleshooting
and make sure

00:14:59.540 --> 00:15:02.180
that the logs that you
expect are there, that things

00:15:02.180 --> 00:15:03.990
are working as expected?

00:15:03.990 --> 00:15:06.860
So there's a lot that has
to change with respect

00:15:06.860 --> 00:15:11.100
to the operations as well.

00:15:11.100 --> 00:15:14.987
And then there is the concept
of pets versus cattle.

00:15:14.987 --> 00:15:16.820
How many of you have
heard this term before,

00:15:16.820 --> 00:15:19.040
pets versus cattle?

00:15:19.040 --> 00:15:22.560
So you have your pets,
your pets at home.

00:15:22.560 --> 00:15:24.540
You take care of them.

00:15:24.540 --> 00:15:25.870
You love them.

00:15:25.870 --> 00:15:28.520
If something's wrong
with them, you go ahead

00:15:28.520 --> 00:15:30.980
and help them feel better.

00:15:30.980 --> 00:15:36.110
And you might put a
little Band-Aid on

00:15:36.110 --> 00:15:39.350
or maybe give them
additional resources.

00:15:39.350 --> 00:15:40.850
And then there's the cattle.

00:15:40.850 --> 00:15:43.520
So when you think about
cattle, each and every cattle

00:15:43.520 --> 00:15:45.780
is identical.

00:15:45.780 --> 00:15:47.227
And if you have
greater workloads,

00:15:47.227 --> 00:15:49.310
you're just going to scale
up the number of cattle

00:15:49.310 --> 00:15:51.840
that you have.

00:15:51.840 --> 00:15:55.260
So that's a
philosophy difference.

00:15:55.260 --> 00:15:57.620
And of course, you can have
either one in the cloud.

00:15:57.620 --> 00:16:02.450
You can bring pets, or unique
instances, over into the cloud

00:16:02.450 --> 00:16:04.640
where, let's say,
each individual VM has

00:16:04.640 --> 00:16:07.610
its own configuration and
you need to keep it alive

00:16:07.610 --> 00:16:09.590
and run with only
that VM and not have

00:16:09.590 --> 00:16:11.450
it be automatically deleted.

00:16:11.450 --> 00:16:13.370
You could do that
with a technology,

00:16:13.370 --> 00:16:18.260
such as individual VMs or
unmanaged instance groups.

00:16:18.260 --> 00:16:20.480
But you may want
to take advantage

00:16:20.480 --> 00:16:23.000
of some of the auto-healing
and auto-scaling

00:16:23.000 --> 00:16:26.990
that we had talked about
earlier and then take

00:16:26.990 --> 00:16:30.290
the cattle approach to take
advantage of some platforms

00:16:30.290 --> 00:16:33.080
like, for example,
managed instance groups

00:16:33.080 --> 00:16:36.440
or where it could be scaling
up your pause in Kubernetes

00:16:36.440 --> 00:16:38.062
engine.

00:16:38.062 --> 00:16:39.770
So there's a little
bit of a question of,

00:16:39.770 --> 00:16:42.200
what do your applications
look like today?

00:16:42.200 --> 00:16:44.330
How are you
operationally addressing

00:16:44.330 --> 00:16:47.300
the need for additional
VMs or resources?

00:16:47.300 --> 00:16:53.480
And how can you support
that in GCP or in the cloud?

00:16:53.480 --> 00:16:55.250
So in addition to
that, there's a mapping

00:16:55.250 --> 00:16:57.490
of people and
processes, of course.

00:16:57.490 --> 00:16:59.740
Is everyone comfortable
with supporting everything

00:16:59.740 --> 00:17:01.480
in production in the cloud?

00:17:01.480 --> 00:17:04.450
And looking at your solutions
for things like secrets,

00:17:04.450 --> 00:17:06.742
how are you doing your
secrets management today?

00:17:06.742 --> 00:17:09.200
And how is that going to change
when you move to the cloud?

00:17:09.200 --> 00:17:13.000
How are you managing
your SSL certificates?

00:17:13.000 --> 00:17:15.260
And how is that going to change?

00:17:15.260 --> 00:17:18.500
And the list continues.

00:17:18.500 --> 00:17:22.150
So here's an example
customer on-premise footprint

00:17:22.150 --> 00:17:24.710
that we worked with.

00:17:24.710 --> 00:17:28.190
And they have a number
of different data centers

00:17:28.190 --> 00:17:30.290
in multiple regions
that were globally

00:17:30.290 --> 00:17:34.460
distributed and connected by
point-to-point links or VPN.

00:17:37.390 --> 00:17:39.050
And when they
moved to the cloud,

00:17:39.050 --> 00:17:47.430
the idea was to bring up
adjacent data centers in GCP.

00:17:47.430 --> 00:17:51.210
So what does that look like from
an architectural perspective?

00:17:51.210 --> 00:17:56.120
So here for example, you
have a shared VPC network

00:17:56.120 --> 00:18:01.220
on GCP, which allows you
to have a global network.

00:18:01.220 --> 00:18:04.040
And so if the resources
within your cloud, or the VMs,

00:18:04.040 --> 00:18:07.940
need to communicate, they can
communicate for the services

00:18:07.940 --> 00:18:10.820
on the private IP space.

00:18:10.820 --> 00:18:16.250
And then they wanted
[INAUDIBLE] for the interim

00:18:16.250 --> 00:18:21.800
some tools or applications that
are still located on-premise

00:18:21.800 --> 00:18:25.670
or perhaps some of the data
is still on premise until you

00:18:25.670 --> 00:18:27.620
complete the migration.

00:18:27.620 --> 00:18:32.240
Then in that case, we set
up dedicated interconnects

00:18:32.240 --> 00:18:37.710
between the adjacent regions at
about 10 gigabits per second.

00:18:37.710 --> 00:18:40.962
So that's what the overall
foundation looked like.

00:18:40.962 --> 00:18:43.722
I forgot to mention that,
with the shared VPC,

00:18:43.722 --> 00:18:45.950
the benefit there is
all your resources

00:18:45.950 --> 00:18:47.660
can sit on the same network.

00:18:47.660 --> 00:18:51.440
But each of the services
and the resources

00:18:51.440 --> 00:18:53.540
can sit in their own projects.

00:18:53.540 --> 00:18:59.180
And the access controls can be
controlled at the project level

00:18:59.180 --> 00:19:01.880
while they still leverage
the same network, which

00:19:01.880 --> 00:19:02.920
is beautiful.

00:19:05.560 --> 00:19:10.030
OK, so we kind of talked about
the horizontal architecture.

00:19:10.030 --> 00:19:15.060
Now, lets talk about
our discovery period

00:19:15.060 --> 00:19:18.360
for looking at these
applications and services

00:19:18.360 --> 00:19:19.920
vertically.

00:19:19.920 --> 00:19:22.620
So here we wanted to
take a holistic approach.

00:19:22.620 --> 00:19:25.200
And we learned early
on that, in order

00:19:25.200 --> 00:19:28.380
to take a holistic
approach, we actually

00:19:28.380 --> 00:19:32.250
wanted to lay the foundation,
as we did, and then

00:19:32.250 --> 00:19:36.270
also do a deep dive in every
single application, or at least

00:19:36.270 --> 00:19:37.800
the significant ones.

00:19:37.800 --> 00:19:40.890
So we did this for about
45 or so applications

00:19:40.890 --> 00:19:44.280
vertically asking
questions such as,

00:19:44.280 --> 00:19:47.820
what's the function of this
service at a basic level?

00:19:47.820 --> 00:19:48.900
Who are the consumers?

00:19:48.900 --> 00:19:50.070
Where are they located?

00:19:50.070 --> 00:19:51.930
So that way, we can
make sure that we

00:19:51.930 --> 00:19:54.480
are able to facilitate
the connectivity.

00:19:54.480 --> 00:19:58.440
For example, are the services
communicating internally?

00:19:58.440 --> 00:20:00.690
Are they external consumers?

00:20:00.690 --> 00:20:03.330
Are they on-premises
with the customer?

00:20:03.330 --> 00:20:05.100
Are they within the same region?

00:20:05.100 --> 00:20:07.230
Are they globally located?

00:20:07.230 --> 00:20:09.550
So there's a lot
of variations here.

00:20:09.550 --> 00:20:11.460
And then that also
helped us understand,

00:20:11.460 --> 00:20:14.950
when we would test eventually,
what that would look like.

00:20:14.950 --> 00:20:16.770
And then the other
question was, what

00:20:16.770 --> 00:20:20.970
are the downstream
dependencies from here?

00:20:20.970 --> 00:20:24.120
Who needs to talk
to what downstream?

00:20:24.120 --> 00:20:26.310
And that way, from a
networking perspective,

00:20:26.310 --> 00:20:32.470
we could make sure that it was
set up to enable that as well.

00:20:32.470 --> 00:20:35.160
And then looking at the data
layer, what do the backends

00:20:35.160 --> 00:20:36.660
look like today?

00:20:36.660 --> 00:20:39.450
And on-premises,
are they shared?

00:20:39.450 --> 00:20:42.000
Should they be shared when
they moved to the cloud?

00:20:42.000 --> 00:20:45.170
Or are they actually using
completely different tables

00:20:45.170 --> 00:20:48.840
and it would be OK to split them
up and put the individual data

00:20:48.840 --> 00:20:53.860
instances with the
individual services?

00:20:53.860 --> 00:20:55.240
So a lot of questions there.

00:20:55.240 --> 00:20:58.350
And other questions
such as, does the data

00:20:58.350 --> 00:21:00.240
need to be directly accessible?

00:21:00.240 --> 00:21:03.550
Will it be accessed
from on-prem?

00:21:03.550 --> 00:21:06.630
And additionally speaking, are
there any specific security

00:21:06.630 --> 00:21:07.680
requirements?

00:21:07.680 --> 00:21:09.630
Because you may
not discover them

00:21:09.630 --> 00:21:11.940
when looking at the
entire architecture.

00:21:11.940 --> 00:21:13.980
But when doing a deep
dive on the service,

00:21:13.980 --> 00:21:16.230
you come across some
of these use cases,

00:21:16.230 --> 00:21:19.620
like perhaps you have services
communicating internally

00:21:19.620 --> 00:21:23.280
and they're using HTTPS.

00:21:23.280 --> 00:21:25.710
And the other question
is, at the deepest level,

00:21:25.710 --> 00:21:29.520
how are your health checks on
your services working today?

00:21:29.520 --> 00:21:34.350
How do you identify when
a service is unhealthy?

00:21:34.350 --> 00:21:37.350
Are you just looking at
the HTTPS response code?

00:21:37.350 --> 00:21:40.030
Are you looking for
that plus a string?

00:21:40.030 --> 00:21:42.000
How does that work in GCP?

00:21:42.000 --> 00:21:46.465
Do our health checks in GCP
work exactly the same way?

00:21:46.465 --> 00:21:48.840
So these are just an example
or a subset of the questions

00:21:48.840 --> 00:21:51.390
that we looked at for
each of the services

00:21:51.390 --> 00:21:58.350
to make sure that we recognized
what was unknown early on

00:21:58.350 --> 00:22:03.420
and identified it so
that we could look at it.

00:22:03.420 --> 00:22:05.400
So other results
of the discovery--

00:22:05.400 --> 00:22:06.970
what came out of it?

00:22:06.970 --> 00:22:11.700
So after going through
this deep dive discovery,

00:22:11.700 --> 00:22:16.320
we actually fed the results back
into the overall architecture

00:22:16.320 --> 00:22:21.330
and adjusted our architecture
accordingly, developed criteria

00:22:21.330 --> 00:22:25.230
for determining on a per
service basis which load bounds

00:22:25.230 --> 00:22:26.850
we should use, for example--

00:22:26.850 --> 00:22:28.950
because there are a
few different flavors

00:22:28.950 --> 00:22:31.470
within the platform--

00:22:31.470 --> 00:22:34.410
determining how we're
going to do DNS resolution

00:22:34.410 --> 00:22:38.260
during the split brain
period, figuring out

00:22:38.260 --> 00:22:44.460
other things such as how are
we going to do our secrets

00:22:44.460 --> 00:22:47.370
management, how are
we going to split up

00:22:47.370 --> 00:22:50.400
or data instances for
each of the services.

00:22:50.400 --> 00:22:52.770
Are we going to put one
service in per project?

00:22:52.770 --> 00:22:58.290
Is this currently shared data
going to be split up into two?

00:22:58.290 --> 00:22:59.550
So we went from there.

00:22:59.550 --> 00:23:01.680
And one of the most
important things

00:23:01.680 --> 00:23:05.250
that we came up with was
a blueprint, a blueprint

00:23:05.250 --> 00:23:10.710
for, if you are going to
lift and shift application Y,

00:23:10.710 --> 00:23:12.780
per this customer's
requirements,

00:23:12.780 --> 00:23:15.630
and their architecture, and
the individual applications'

00:23:15.630 --> 00:23:18.867
requirements, what
does that look like?

00:23:18.867 --> 00:23:19.700
What's the criteria?

00:23:19.700 --> 00:23:21.283
And what are all the
decisions that we

00:23:21.283 --> 00:23:23.830
need to make when we're
doing that lift and shift?

00:23:23.830 --> 00:23:26.160
So we came up with a
comprehensive blueprint

00:23:26.160 --> 00:23:28.650
that we could follow
to really scale

00:23:28.650 --> 00:23:33.170
when we're working
with 10's and 50

00:23:33.170 --> 00:23:36.135
to 70 or so microservices,
which was really key.

00:23:36.135 --> 00:23:38.010
So that way, when they're
lifted and shifted,

00:23:38.010 --> 00:23:41.970
they're lifted and
shifted consistently.

00:23:41.970 --> 00:23:44.070
Other things that
we looked at was

00:23:44.070 --> 00:23:46.800
we took their
on-premise load balancer

00:23:46.800 --> 00:23:49.590
and did a deep dive and
analysis on the features

00:23:49.590 --> 00:23:52.530
and capabilities there
that were being used today

00:23:52.530 --> 00:23:58.650
to see how those would map over
to capabilities in the cloud.

00:23:58.650 --> 00:24:03.150
And then we also looked
at how individual

00:24:03.150 --> 00:24:05.070
teams would access
the applications

00:24:05.070 --> 00:24:06.960
and how we provisioned that.

00:24:06.960 --> 00:24:10.650
And the biggest piece
that came out of this

00:24:10.650 --> 00:24:12.630
was the known unknown.

00:24:12.630 --> 00:24:14.820
So you have the
unknown unknown, which

00:24:14.820 --> 00:24:18.240
is kind of like this
nebulous thing, which is just

00:24:18.240 --> 00:24:19.790
like this horrible feeling.

00:24:19.790 --> 00:24:21.450
But at least at
the end of this, we

00:24:21.450 --> 00:24:24.050
had surfaced a number
of known unknowns

00:24:24.050 --> 00:24:26.790
that then we could
take and tackle

00:24:26.790 --> 00:24:32.535
and solution for so that way we
didn't have surprises later on.

00:24:32.535 --> 00:24:36.320
So that was a key result
that really came out of this.

00:24:41.030 --> 00:24:43.640
And now, let's move to
deployment strategy,

00:24:43.640 --> 00:24:46.610
now that we talked about
lifting is shifting

00:24:46.610 --> 00:24:49.050
the individual applications.

00:24:49.050 --> 00:24:51.110
So there's a couple of
options here, right?

00:24:51.110 --> 00:24:53.840
When you're going to
deploy into the cloud,

00:24:53.840 --> 00:24:56.740
you can either take
everything that you have,

00:24:56.740 --> 00:25:00.080
spin up some VMs,
spin up a platform,

00:25:00.080 --> 00:25:04.410
move your data over, move your
image over, get things going,

00:25:04.410 --> 00:25:06.530
and you're on your way, right?

00:25:06.530 --> 00:25:13.550
You can start testing and
then get to Production

00:25:13.550 --> 00:25:19.100
The other option there
is to automate it,

00:25:19.100 --> 00:25:20.870
with a lot of
additional benefits.

00:25:20.870 --> 00:25:22.610
Such as, if you automate--

00:25:22.610 --> 00:25:25.340
in this scenario, there
was multiple data centers

00:25:25.340 --> 00:25:27.830
and we wanted to deploy
consistently across each

00:25:27.830 --> 00:25:29.180
of the data centers.

00:25:29.180 --> 00:25:32.040
So by automating it and
taking an automated approach

00:25:32.040 --> 00:25:34.310
with terraform, we
could consistently

00:25:34.310 --> 00:25:37.410
do that across four
different data centers.

00:25:37.410 --> 00:25:39.950
So what might be some
initial investment

00:25:39.950 --> 00:25:45.590
in spending the time to code
resulted in longer term speed

00:25:45.590 --> 00:25:48.590
and in consistency
and simplicity.

00:25:48.590 --> 00:25:51.570
And then also, if there was
ever an emergency scenario

00:25:51.570 --> 00:25:54.907
and you needed it
to do a redeploy,

00:25:54.907 --> 00:25:56.990
you could go ahead and do
the redeploy because you

00:25:56.990 --> 00:25:58.160
have the code there.

00:25:58.160 --> 00:26:00.410
You're not worried about
sitting there and spinning up

00:26:00.410 --> 00:26:02.280
everything individually.

00:26:02.280 --> 00:26:05.660
So this approach nicely lent
itself to multi-data center

00:26:05.660 --> 00:26:06.289
deployments.

00:26:06.289 --> 00:26:07.830
And that's the
approach that we took.

00:26:10.931 --> 00:26:13.590
OK, so now the
testing approach--

00:26:13.590 --> 00:26:18.150
the big question in testing
in this scenario was--

00:26:18.150 --> 00:26:20.070
there was everything
on-premises.

00:26:20.070 --> 00:26:23.820
There was a global mesh
of services, each of which

00:26:23.820 --> 00:26:26.940
were being deployed in every
single region for the most

00:26:26.940 --> 00:26:28.290
part.

00:26:28.290 --> 00:26:32.490
The question is, do we
test in a hybrid mode,

00:26:32.490 --> 00:26:35.130
meaning that we
bring up one service

00:26:35.130 --> 00:26:38.550
and then do integration
testing with the other services

00:26:38.550 --> 00:26:42.870
in on-premise to verify that
it's working as expected?

00:26:42.870 --> 00:26:49.730
Or do we go ahead and come
up with the full stack in GCP

00:26:49.730 --> 00:26:50.730
and then do our testing?

00:26:53.550 --> 00:26:57.840
So by taking one service and
bringing it up and testing

00:26:57.840 --> 00:27:01.420
with on-prem, that required
a few different things.

00:27:01.420 --> 00:27:08.220
And one is reconfiguring the
INT environment on-premise.

00:27:08.220 --> 00:27:10.920
So we're polluting
that environment.

00:27:10.920 --> 00:27:14.100
And the second downfall
there is that you're actually

00:27:14.100 --> 00:27:16.860
not testing what's going
to go in production.

00:27:16.860 --> 00:27:18.870
You're testing a
different configuration

00:27:18.870 --> 00:27:22.720
than will actually be deployed
at the end of the day.

00:27:22.720 --> 00:27:25.290
So you may miss things in
terms of what's not really

00:27:25.290 --> 00:27:28.410
working together from an
integration perspective.

00:27:28.410 --> 00:27:33.540
So for those reasons,
we took the approach

00:27:33.540 --> 00:27:41.150
of actually testing, doing
development in parallel,

00:27:41.150 --> 00:27:46.170
bringing up each of the
services, doing unit testing,

00:27:46.170 --> 00:27:49.950
and then going ahead and
testing in full stack.

00:27:49.950 --> 00:27:52.170
Because at the end
of the day, that

00:27:52.170 --> 00:27:56.970
is what will be
deployed in production.

00:27:56.970 --> 00:28:00.200
And you have a clear
entrance and exit criteria in

00:28:00.200 --> 00:28:03.000
can know, "Yeah, we
should be good to go.

00:28:03.000 --> 00:28:05.666
This will be working when
we get to production."

00:28:08.930 --> 00:28:11.390
Now, I wanted to talk
about the path forward.

00:28:11.390 --> 00:28:16.060
So after going through all of
that, what came out of this?

00:28:16.060 --> 00:28:18.940
Some new guiding principles--

00:28:18.940 --> 00:28:21.890
the first being lift and shift--

00:28:21.890 --> 00:28:25.370
it's really critical to
have a discussion early on

00:28:25.370 --> 00:28:29.240
and define and make sure
everyone is on the same page

00:28:29.240 --> 00:28:32.950
as far as what a shift
in shift means to them.

00:28:32.950 --> 00:28:35.630
Is it the lift and shift in the
purest form, a lift and shift

00:28:35.630 --> 00:28:39.650
with some transformation, a
complete replatform, a lift

00:28:39.650 --> 00:28:42.470
and shift and then a transform?

00:28:42.470 --> 00:28:43.670
What does it mean?

00:28:43.670 --> 00:28:47.840
And then as you're going
through your migration process,

00:28:47.840 --> 00:28:50.720
when you come to a
decision, look back and say,

00:28:50.720 --> 00:28:52.220
"Are we staying true to that?"

00:28:52.220 --> 00:28:56.960
Or maybe you need to pivot
on that initial decision,

00:28:56.960 --> 00:29:00.530
and really writing that
down and taking a look at it

00:29:00.530 --> 00:29:02.150
every now and then.

00:29:02.150 --> 00:29:04.757
The other is managed
service preferred, right?

00:29:04.757 --> 00:29:06.590
You get all the beautiful
bells and whistles

00:29:06.590 --> 00:29:10.230
when you're using
a managed service.

00:29:10.230 --> 00:29:13.250
So really, that's
a nice way to go,

00:29:13.250 --> 00:29:16.070
as long as your application
will fit in it, right?

00:29:16.070 --> 00:29:19.490
In order for you to
use a managed service--

00:29:19.490 --> 00:29:21.620
if you want to
drive the shiny car,

00:29:21.620 --> 00:29:23.890
your application
needs to fit in it.

00:29:23.890 --> 00:29:27.220
So it's just verifying that
you meet that criteria.

00:29:27.220 --> 00:29:29.910
And the other is to take
a holistic approach.

00:29:29.910 --> 00:29:33.860
So think about the architecture
at a horizontal level,

00:29:33.860 --> 00:29:38.020
but then also do the diligence
to do the vertical deep dive.

00:29:38.020 --> 00:29:42.800
So that way, you can foresee
what the known unknowns are

00:29:42.800 --> 00:29:45.020
and discover anything
legacy that's

00:29:45.020 --> 00:29:47.150
sitting there that you
may need to do a deeper

00:29:47.150 --> 00:29:49.910
investigation on.

00:29:49.910 --> 00:29:52.550
And the fourth, as far
as deployment strategy,

00:29:52.550 --> 00:29:54.290
is especially when
you're dealing

00:29:54.290 --> 00:29:59.110
with a complex environment
with multiple data centers

00:29:59.110 --> 00:30:01.730
and if you want a way to
easily redeploy in an emergency

00:30:01.730 --> 00:30:06.020
scenario, spend the
time and automate

00:30:06.020 --> 00:30:12.200
to move faster over time,
so automate to move fast.

00:30:12.200 --> 00:30:13.730
And as far as a
testing approach,

00:30:13.730 --> 00:30:16.550
it's a full stack
wherever possible

00:30:16.550 --> 00:30:20.150
or whatever is going to look
closest to your production

00:30:20.150 --> 00:30:21.330
environment.

00:30:21.330 --> 00:30:23.270
And in this case, that
was full stack testing

00:30:23.270 --> 00:30:24.680
as opposed to hybrid testing.

00:30:27.530 --> 00:30:31.770
Great, I'm going to call my
colleague Dillon back up here.

00:30:31.770 --> 00:30:36.980
And this is a large part of
the team that was working

00:30:36.980 --> 00:30:38.680
on this migration project.

00:30:38.680 --> 00:30:39.990
It was an entire team.

00:30:39.990 --> 00:30:41.930
And honestly, there's
many more people, too.

00:30:41.930 --> 00:30:45.250
We couldn't fit
everyone on the slide.

00:30:45.250 --> 00:30:47.080
I mean, it's really
a big team effort.

00:30:47.080 --> 00:30:49.640
There's a lot of moving
parts and a lot of expertise

00:30:49.640 --> 00:30:51.740
needed to lift and shift.

00:30:51.740 --> 00:30:58.990
[MUSIC PLAYING]

